  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 110 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nWednesday night through Thursday night\nAssessment...very low (10-15%) confidence of minor impacts from a\nrain/snow mix or light snow\n\nWednesday night into Thursday evening looks interesting.\n\nTwo scenarios are in play: Scenario 1 (no phasing) would keep\nmost of the area dry with precipitation well to the east of the\narea. Scenario 2 (phasing) would result in precipitation over \nthe area with the better chances east of the Mississippi.\n\nThe global models have been vacillating with each run regarding \nwhether or not any phasing occurs. The newest suite of runs has \ntilted toward phasing. The deterministic runs show phasing \n(GFS, ECMWF, CMC, UKMET) to varying amounts or no phasing \n(ICON). AIFS/AIGFS are mixed as are the respective ensembles \nfrom the various global models regarding phasing.\n\nWhere there is agreement is with the mean QPF of the ensembles;\nthe heavier QPF grazes the eastern third of the area; \nespecially east of a line from Elizabeth, IL to Galva, IL.\n\nForecast soundings and wet bulb temperatures are supportive of a \nrain/snow mix with dynamic cooling potentially resulting in a change \nover to all snow during the night. Interestingly, 925 mb temperatures\nin all models are forecast to slowly rise to or above freezing \nlate Wednesday night into Thursday suggesting to change back\nover to eventually rain.\n\nThus the question is will there be any impacts from any snow.\n\nThe BIG negatives for accumulations are the very warm ground (45+ \ndegrees) and still very warm road temperatures. Such warm \ntemperatures would result in melting on contact. If areas of all \nsnow occur, snowfall rates would need to be an inch per hour AND \noccurring at night in order to see even minor accumulations on \ngrassy and elevated surfaces.\n\nThus the overall potential for minor impacts is very low (10-15%). \nThe probability of seeing an inch of snow east of the Mississippi is \n10-15 percent in spots with most areas 5 percent or less.",
